  "content": {
    "id": "p21-q20-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Suppose that $x$ is a positive rational number. Prove that if $x^2+x\\in\\mathbb{Z}$, then $x\\in\\mathbb{Z}$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$x\\in\\mathbb{Z}$.\n\nApply the Integer Root Theorem to the monic polynomial $P(t)=t^2+t-n$, where $n=x^2+x\\in\\mathbb{Z}$.",
      "worked": "Let $n=x^2+x\\in\\mathbb{Z}$. Since $x>0$, we have $n>0$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2+x&=n\\\\x^2+x-n&=0.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThus $x$ is a rational root of $P(t)=t^2+t-n$, a monic polynomial with integer coefficients and non-zero constant term. By the Integer Root Theorem, $x\\in\\mathbb{Z}$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 30
  },
